<!--
  Ubiquiti UniFi access points and switches (UniFi Network remote syslog)

  On the wire the device sends RFC 3164 syslog:

    <14>Aug 20 09:11:17 U7-Pro-Outdoor 9041b21628d1,U7-Pro-Outdoor-8.6.11+18870: hostapd[13361]: wifi1ap4: AP-STA-CONNECTED 22:94:04:5f:25:58

  wazuh-remoted strips the priority, then pre-decoding takes the hostname and
  tries to read a program name. It fails, and that failure is what shapes this
  whole file:

    cleanevent.c walks the program-name candidate with isValidChar(), whose
    hostname_map marks "," as invalid (os_regex_maps.c). The candidate is
    "9041b21628d1", the character that stops the walk is "," - not ":", not "["
    and not "|" - so the last else branch sets program_name = NULL and leaves
    lf->log pointing at the whole remainder.

  The decoders therefore see:

    program_name = (none)
    log          = 9041b21628d1,U7-Pro-Outdoor-8.6.11+18870: hostapd[13361]: wifi1ap4: AP-STA-CONNECTED 22:94:04:5f:25:58

  This is the opposite of the ExtremeXOS case. Wazuh keeps decoders with and
  without program_name in separate lists and searches only the one matching the
  pre-decoded event, so here the parent must NOT declare program_name - and the
  device MAC, model, firmware and daemon name stay inside the body where the
  decoders can extract them.

  Constraints from analysisd (decoder.c, decoders_list.c, shared/expression.c):

  1. A parent that has children never runs its own regex. All fields come from
     the children.
  2. Decoders cannot have grandchildren: a child decoder cannot be a parent.
     Every unifi-device decoder is therefore a direct child of symantec-av.
  3. Children that share one name are chained: analysisd walks the whole chain
     and applies every regex that matches, skipping the ones that do not.
     Children with distinct names are not chained - the first whose prematch
     matches is picked, and if its regex fails the event is dropped undecoded.
     Every child here is named "unifi-device" for that reason. Children after
     the first must not declare prematch. The first child must contain both a
     prematch and a regex so Wazuh can link the following same-name children.
  4. Because the whole chain is applied, one child can carry the header for
     every line while the others add only their own event fields. That is what
     the first child below does, so no event pattern has to repeat the
     "mac,model-firmware: daemon[pid]:" prefix. Patterns must still be mutually
     exclusive per field, or the same field would be set twice.
  5. PCRE2 groups are read up to the highest group that participated in the
     match, and a group that did not participate is read from an unset offset
     (w_expression_PCRE2_fill_regex_match). Optional capture groups are
     therefore only ever placed last.

  Wazuh replaces a child's name with the parent's unless <use_own_name> is set,
  so wazuh-logtest always reports "unifi-device" in Phase 2 and
  "decoded_as unifi-device" matches every child.

  The parent prematch here is more specific than the stock symantec-av decoder,
  but that does not help on its own. Both live in the no-program_name list and
  analysisd stops at the first prematch that matches; the stock file loads
  first. Its prematch is twelve word characters and a comma - identical to a
  UniFi MAC prefix - so a standalone unifi-device parent is unreachable
  (wazuh-ruleset#840). The decoder below is therefore a child of symantec-av.
  Do not install a custom Symantec replacement that narrows the stock parent
  prematch: UniFi would no longer reach this child chain. See README.

  Install: copy to /var/ossec/etc/decoders/ on the manager, then restart
  wazuh-manager.
-->

<!--
  UniFi AP/switch logs match the stock symantec-av prematch
  (`^\w\w\w\w\w\w\w\w\w\w\w\w,`) before this file is even searched —
  wazuh-ruleset#840. analysisd stops at the first parent, so a standalone
  unifi-device parent is unreachable no matter how specific its prematch is.

  The only reliable fix is for the complete same-name sibling chain to consist
  of direct children of that parent. use_own_name keeps decoded_as as
  unifi-device so the rules still key on it. The first child prematch requires
  the firmware token after the comma (`U7-Pro-8.7.11+19419:`), which Symantec
  CSV (`24090D00000A,4,3,7,...`) never has, so real Symantec events are left on
  the parent. This first child also carries the first regex; Wazuh requires it
  in order to link the following same-name children as a multi-regex chain.
-->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <use_own_name>true</use_own_name>
  <prematch type="pcre2">^[0-9a-f]{12},[\w.+-]+: </prematch>
  <regex type="pcre2">^([0-9a-f]{12}),([\w-]+)-(\d+\.\d+\.\d+)\+(\d+): (?:switch: |: )?([\w.-]+)(?:\[\d+\])?: (.*)$</regex>
  <order>unifi.mac,unifi.model,unifi.firmware,unifi.build,unifi.daemon,unifi.message</order>
</decoder>

<!--
  The first decoder above extracts the normal header and is applied to every
  line of this family and nothing else. It guarantees
  unifi.daemon and unifi.message, so an event whose message text drifts between
  firmware releases is still attributed to a device and a daemon instead of
  vanishing. The optional groups here are non-capturing, so they do not shift
  PCRE2 numbering: "switch: " is the extra slot the switch platform inserts
  before the daemon, and ": " is that same slot left empty.

  The model is matched with [\w-]+ rather than .+ on purpose. A greedy .+ can
  cross the ": " that ends the prefix and re-satisfy the rest of the pattern
  further along the line, because a message body may itself contain a
  version-like token; [\w-]+ cannot cross a colon or a space, so the prefix
  boundary is the only place this can match.
-->

<!--
  Header fallback, for a firmware whose version token is not
  "<model>-<x.y.z>+<build>". Without it such a line would reach no child at all
  and be reported as undecoded. The negative lookahead makes it mutually
  exclusive with the pattern above, so unifi.mac and unifi.message are never
  set twice on one line.
-->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">^([0-9a-f]{12}),(?![\w-]+-\d+\.\d+\.\d+\+\d+: )([\w.+-]+): (?:switch: |: )?([\w.-]+)(?:\[\d+\])?: (.*)$</regex>
  <order>unifi.mac,unifi.firmware,unifi.daemon,unifi.message</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- hostapd - association lifecycle                                      -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.11: associated (aid <n>) -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.11: (associated) \(aid (\d+)\)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.aid</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.11: disassociated -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.11: (disassociated)$</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.11: deauthenticated due to local deauth request -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.11: (deauthenticated) due to (.+)$</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.reason</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd control interface: AP-STA-CONNECTED <mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): (AP-STA-CONNECTED) ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,id,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd control interface: AP-STA-DISCONNECTED <mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): (AP-STA-DISCONNECTED) ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,id,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: authorized - the 4-way handshake completed -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) WPA: (authorized)$</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- hostapd - authentication failures                                    -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!--
  hostapd control interface: AP-STA-POSSIBLE-PSK-MISMATCH <mac>
  Emitted from wpa_auth.c when the 4-way handshake times out after the station
  answered message 1, which is what a wrong pre-shared key looks like.
-->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): (AP-STA-POSSIBLE-PSK-MISMATCH) ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,id,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: EAPOL-Key timeout / 4-way handshake failure -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) WPA: (group key handshake failed|4-Way Handshake failed|EAPOL-Key timeout)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: received EAPOL-Key with invalid MIC -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) WPA: received EAPOL-Key with (invalid MIC)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.11: Michael MIC failure detected -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.11: (Michael MIC failure) detected</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.11: Station not allowed by MAC ACL -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.11: (deny|Station) (?:not allowed|MAC address not in the accept list)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> IEEE 802.1X: authentication failed / EAP failure -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) IEEE 802\.1X: (authentication failed)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- hostapd - RADIUS                                                     -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> RADIUS: starting accounting session <session> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) RADIUS: (starting accounting session) (\S+)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.session</order>
</decoder>

<!--
  hostapd: RADIUS: No response from Authentication server <ip>:<port>

  The interface prefix hostapd puts in front of this message is deliberately
  not captured. It is present on some builds and absent on others, and a
  leading optional capture group is the one PCRE2 shape Wazuh cannot read: it
  would take every later group from an unset offset whenever the prefix was
  missing. The RADIUS server address is what the event is about anyway.
-->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">RADIUS: (No response from Authentication server) ((?:\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}):(\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,dstip,dstport</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- hostapd - Ubiquiti roaming extension                                 -->
<!--                                                                      -->
<!-- UBNT_ROAM is a Ubiquiti patch, not upstream hostapd, so these three   -->
<!-- patterns come from the capture rather than from hostapd source. They  -->
<!-- are the bulk of the traffic in a roaming-heavy network and are        -->
<!-- decoded mainly so they can be given a low-noise rule.                -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: Receive UBNT_ROAM from <peer ap mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) WPA: (Receive UBNT_ROAM) from ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.peer_ap</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: UBNT_ROAM received: STA roamed to peer AP <mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) WPA: (UBNT_ROAM received): STA roamed to peer AP ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.peer_ap</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: <vap>: UBNT ROAM: STA=<mac> CurrentAP=<mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA (?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2} WPA: \S+: (UBNT ROAM): STA=((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) CurrentAP=((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,id,unifi.sta_mac,unifi.peer_ap</order>
</decoder>

<!-- hostapd: STA <mac> WPA: <vap>: immed  disassoc for <mac> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">hostapd\[\d+\]: (\S+): STA (?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2} WPA: \S+: (immed) +disassoc for ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,id,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- stahtd - Ubiquiti station tracking, JSON payload                     -->
<!--                                                                      -->
<!-- The richest source of client-side security signal on a UniFi AP: it   -->
<!-- reports association outcome, roaming and per-station anomalies. Key   -->
<!-- order inside the object is stable per event_type but the spacing      -->
<!-- after a colon is not, so every pattern allows optional whitespace.    -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- stahtd: {"op":"event","message_type":"STA_ASSOC_TRACKER","event_type":"...","mac":"...","vap":"...","assoc_status":"..."} -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">stahtd\[\d+\]: .*"message_type":\s*"([^"]+)","event_type":\s*"([^"]+)","mac":\s*"([^"]+)","vap":\s*"([^"]+)","assoc_status":\s*"([^"]+)"</regex>
  <order>unifi.message_type,id,unifi.sta_mac,unifi.vap,unifi.assoc_status</order>
</decoder>

<!-- stahtd: the auth_algo / auth_rssi tail present on association events -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">stahtd\[\d+\]: .*"auth_algo":\s*"([^"]+)","auth_rssi":\s*"(-?\d+)"</regex>
  <order>unifi.auth_algo,unifi.rssi</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- wevent - Ubiquiti client join/leave/IP events                        -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- wevent: EVENT_STA_JOIN <vap>: <mac> / <n> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">wevent\[\d+\]: wevent\.ubnt_custom_event\(\): (EVENT_STA_JOIN) (\S+): ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- wevent: EVENT_STA_LEAVE <vap>: <mac> / <n> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">wevent\[\d+\]: wevent\.ubnt_custom_event\(\): (EVENT_STA_LEAVE) (\S+): ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac</order>
</decoder>

<!-- wevent: EVENT_STA_IP <vap>: <mac> / <ip> - binds a client MAC to an address -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">wevent\[\d+\]: wevent\.ubnt_custom_event\(\): (EVENT_STA_IP) (\S+): ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2}) / ((?:\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3})</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,srcip</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- kernel - driver and station tracker                                  -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- kernel: [STA_TRACKER] DNS request timed out; [STA: <mac>] [DNS_TIMEOUTS: <n>] -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">kernel: \[STA_TRACKER\] (DNS request timed out); \[STA: ((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})\] \[DNS_TIMEOUTS: (\d+)\]</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.sta_mac,unifi.count</order>
</decoder>

<!-- kernel: wlan: ... vap-<n>(<vap>): [<mac>]recv auth frame with algorithm <n> seq <n> rssi:<n> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">kernel: wlan: .*vap-\d+\((\S+)\): \[((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})\](recv auth frame) with algorithm (\d+) seq (\d+) rssi:(\d+)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.auth_algo,unifi.auth_seq,unifi.rssi</order>
</decoder>

<!-- kernel: wlan: ... vap-<n>(<vap>): [<mac>]station associated at aid <n>: <caps> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">kernel: wlan: .*vap-\d+\((\S+)\): \[((?:[0-9a-f]{2}:){5}[0-9a-f]{2})\](station associated) at aid (\d+)</regex>
  <order>unifi.vap,unifi.sta_mac,id,unifi.aid</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- switch platform - TRAPMGR / DOT1S                                    -->
<!--                                                                      -->
<!-- UniFi switches wrap their firmware log as "switch: <COMPONENT>: msg". -->
<!-- Note this is NOT the EdgeSwitch/FASTPATH shape, which carries a       -->
<!-- "traputil.c(735) 248 %%" prefix instead; EdgeSwitch needs its own     -->
<!-- file, because there the bracket holds a task name rather than a pid   -->
<!-- and pre-decoding lands it in a different decoder list.                -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- switch: TRAPMGR: Link Up: <unit>/<port> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: TRAPMGR: (Link Up): (\d+/\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.port</order>
</decoder>

<!-- switch: TRAPMGR: Link Down: <unit>/<port> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: TRAPMGR: (Link Down): (\d+/\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.port</order>
</decoder>

<!-- switch: TRAPMGR: SFP inserted in <unit>/<port> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: TRAPMGR: (SFP inserted) in (\d+/\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.port</order>
</decoder>

<!-- switch: TRAPMGR: SFP removed from <unit>/<port> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: TRAPMGR: (SFP removed) from (\d+/\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.port</order>
</decoder>

<!-- switch: TRAPMGR: Session <n> of type <n> started/ended for user <user> connected from <ip> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: TRAPMGR: Session \d+ of type \d+ (started|ended) for user (\S+?) connected from ((?:\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3})</regex>
  <order>id,srcuser,srcip</order>
</decoder>

<!-- switch: DOT1S: Port (<n>) inst(<n>) role changing from <role> to <role> -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">switch: DOT1S: Port \((\d+)\) inst\((\d+)\) (role changing) from (ROLE_\w+) to (ROLE_\w+)</regex>
  <order>unifi.port,unifi.stp_instance,id,unifi.role_from,unifi.role_to</order>
</decoder>

<!-- ==================================================================== -->
<!-- platform hardware                                                    -->
<!-- ==================================================================== -->

<!-- ubnt-fanctrl: Fan speed <n>% | pwm: ... | fan rpm: <n> | sensor PSU temp: <n>C -->
<decoder name="unifi-device">
  <parent>symantec-av</parent>
  <regex type="pcre2">ubnt-fanctrl\[\d+\]: fanctrl\.fanctrl_log\(\): (Fan speed) (\d+)%.*fan rpm: (\d+)</regex>
  <order>id,unifi.fan_percent,unifi.fan_rpm</order>
</decoder>
